# Solvers

Basin ships a large catalog of optimization solvers driven by the same
`Executor` loop, plus a direct scalar root-finding API. Pick an optimization
solver based on what your problem provides (a gradient? only costs? residuals?)
and whether it is constrained.

Each algorithm name links to its API page on [docs.rs](https://docs.rs/basin);
the trailing note cites the paper it implements (or the source it ports).

## Root Finding

- **[`BrentRoot`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/brent/struct.BrentRoot.html)**:
  bracketed scalar root finding by bisection, secant steps, and inverse
  quadratic interpolation (Brent, 1971). It uses a direct fallible-closure API
  rather than `Executor`, preserving the signed function value and final
  bracket in a root-specific result.
- **[`SecantRoot`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/secant/struct.SecantRoot.html)**:
  secant iteration with bracket preservation and bisection safeguards (SciPy
  1.16.3, `scipy.optimize.newton`, secant update; Basin adds bracketing and
  bracket-based termination).
- **[`NewtonRoot`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/newton/struct.NewtonRoot.html)**:
  safeguarded Newton iteration using first derivatives (Boost.Math 1.89.0,
  "Root Finding With Derivatives: Newton-Raphson, Halley & Schroeder"; Basin
  adds secant fallback, a two-trial contraction safeguard, and bracket-based
  termination).
- **[`HalleyRoot`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/halley/struct.HalleyRoot.html)**:
  safeguarded Halley iteration using first and second derivatives (Boost.Math
  1.89.0, "Root Finding With Derivatives: Newton-Raphson, Halley & Schroeder";
  Basin adds secant fallback, a two-trial contraction safeguard, and
  bracket-based termination).
- **[`Toms748Root`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/root/toms748/struct.Toms748Root.html)**:
  derivative-free bracketed root finding using Algorithm 4.2 (`k = 2`,
  `μ = 0.5`) from Alefeld, Potra, and Shi (1995), "Algorithm 748: Enclosing
  Zeros of Continuous Functions," _ACM Transactions on Mathematical Software_,
  21(3), 327–344, [doi:10.1145/210089.210111](https://doi.org/10.1145/210089.210111).

All five use direct fallible callbacks and preserve signed function values and
the final bracket. Newton and Halley accept separate or combined derivative
callbacks. Convergence requires an exact zero or a sufficiently narrow bracket.

[`RootBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/root/struct.RootBracketer.html)
and
[`MinimumBracketer`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/bracket/minimum/struct.MinimumBracketer.html)
provide automatic geometric bracket searches with optional domain limits.
Bracketing is a separate stage with its own evaluation counts. Pass a root
bracket to a root solver, or put a minimum bracket's outer endpoints on the
problem's `BoxConstraints` and use its middle point to seed a scalar minimizer.
Both searches follow the scalar recurrences in SciPy 1.16.3's elementwise
bracketing routines.

## First-Order

- **[`NonlinearCg`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nonlinear_cg/struct.NonlinearCg.html)**:
  nonlinear conjugate gradient with selectable Hager–Zhang (default) and
  Polak–Ribière+ updates, O(n) storage, a pluggable line search, and optional
  periodic restarts. Select PR+ with
  `with_update(CgUpdate::PolakRibierePlus)`; it truncates negative coefficients
  to zero and restarts when the next direction fails its descent safeguard.
  Selecting an update leaves the line search unchanged. This minimizes smooth
  unconstrained objectives; Steihaug's linear CG solves a trust-region subproblem.
  References: William W. Hager and Hongchao Zhang,
  “A new conjugate gradient method with guaranteed descent and an efficient
  line search,” _SIAM Journal on Optimization_ 16(1), 2005, pp. 170–192,
  equations (1.3), (1.5), and (1.6),
  [doi:10.1137/030601880](https://doi.org/10.1137/030601880);
  Elijah Polak and Gérard Ribière, “Note sur la convergence de méthodes de
  directions conjuguées,” _Revue française d'informatique et de recherche
  opérationnelle, Série rouge_ 3(R1), 1969, pp. 35–43,
  [doi:10.1051/m2an/196903R100351](https://doi.org/10.1051/m2an/196903R100351);
  and Jean Charles Gilbert and Jorge Nocedal, “Global Convergence Properties of
  Conjugate Gradient Methods for Optimization,” _SIAM Journal on Optimization_
  2(1), 1992, pp. 21–42,
  [doi:10.1137/0802003](https://doi.org/10.1137/0802003).
- **[`GradientDescent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gradient_descent/struct.GradientDescent.html)**:
  steepest descent with a pluggable step rule (`Constant`, `Backtracking`,
  `MoreThuente`, `Wolfe`, or `HagerZhang` line searches) and optional
  heavy-ball momentum (Polyak, 1964).
- **[`Sgd`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/sgd/struct.Sgd.html)**:
  mini-batch stochastic gradient descent with a constant learning rate and
  optional Polyak (1964) heavy-ball momentum.
- **[`ProjectedGradientDescent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/projected_gradient_descent/struct.ProjectedGradientDescent.html)**:
  gradient descent for box-constrained problems, projecting each step back onto
  the feasible box.
- **[`BrentDerivative`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/brent_derivative/struct.BrentDerivative.html)**:
  Brent's method using first derivatives ("dbrent"): 1-D minimization on a
  bracketed interval, the gradient-using sibling of `Brent`/`GoldenSection`
  (uses the sign of `f'` to bracket and secant extrapolation on `f'` to step;
  Brent, 1973, as transcribed in *Numerical Recipes* §10.3).

## Derivative-Free

- **[`NelderMead`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/nelder_mead/struct.NelderMead.html)**:
  the classic reflection, expansion, and contraction simplex; needs only a
  `CostFunction`. Implements Lagarias et al. (1998); `NelderMead::adaptive()`
  uses the dimension-aware coefficients of Gao & Han (2012). Its projected
  variant clamps trial points into box bounds; use `Gbnm` for the full
  Luersen–Le Riche restart algorithm.
- **[`Mads`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/mads/struct.Mads.html)**:
  mesh adaptive direct search (the deterministic OrthoMADS instance): polls a
  positive spanning set of `2n` directions on a shrinking mesh, generated from
  the Halton sequence and a scaled Householder reflection. Unlike Nelder-Mead it
  has a convergence guarantee on nonsmooth or non-continuous objectives, a
  box-constrained variant (`Mads::bounded()`) via the extreme barrier, and a
  nonlinearly-constrained variant (`Mads::constrained()`) handling `c(x) ≤ 0`
  via the progressive barrier (which tolerates an infeasible start). Needs only
  a `CostFunction` (plus `NonlinearInequalityConstraints` for the constrained
  mode). Implements Audet & Dennis (2006), OrthoMADS (Abramson, Audet, Dennis &
  Le Digabel 2009), and the progressive barrier (Audet & Dennis 2009).
- **[`Newuoa`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/newuoa/struct.Newuoa.html)**:
  Powell's model-based trust-region method: maintains a quadratic surrogate
  interpolating the objective on `2n+1` points and updates it by the
  least-Frobenius-norm rule, so each iteration needs only one new value. Needs
  only a `CostFunction`. Implements Powell (2006), cross-validated against PRIMA
  v0.7.2.
- **[`Bobyqa`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bobyqa/struct.Bobyqa.html)**:
  the bound-constrained sibling of `Newuoa`: the same least-Frobenius-norm
  quadratic model, with a box-aware trust-region step (TRSBOX) and geometry step
  (ALTMOV), plus the RESCUE restoration procedure. Needs a `CostFunction` and
  `BoxConstraints`. Implements Powell (2009), cross-validated against PRIMA
  v0.7.2.
- **[`Lincoa`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/lincoa/struct.Lincoa.html)**:
  the linearly-constrained sibling of `Newuoa`: the same least-Frobenius-norm
  quadratic model, with a projected truncated-CG trust-region step (TRSTEP) and
  active-set QR (GETACT) that keep every iterate feasible under `A x ≤ b`. Needs
  a `CostFunction` and `LinearConstraints` (the general linear form: box bounds,
  equalities, and inequalities, all folded into `A x ≤ b`). Implements Powell
  (2015), ported from PRIMA v0.7.2.
- **[`Cobyla`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/cobyla/struct.Cobyla.html)**:
  Powell's nonlinearly-constrained solver: it builds **linear** models by
  interpolation at the `n+1` vertices of a simplex and steers by an L-infinity
  exact-penalty merit function. For nonlinear inequalities `c(x) ≤ 0`, supply a
  `CostFunction` and `NonlinearInequalityConstraints`. For nonlinear inequalities
  with optional nonlinear equalities, linear constraints, and box bounds,
  implement
  [`NonlinearConstraints`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/constraint/trait.NonlinearConstraints.html)
  and pass
  [`FoldedConstraints::new(problem)`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/constraint/folded/struct.FoldedConstraints.html)
  to the executor with `Cobyla::new()`. The adapter folds the full feasible set
  into inequalities while retaining Basin's interpolation algorithm. Bound
  constraints do not guarantee feasible callbacks, and radius convergence does
  not certify feasibility. Implements Powell (1994), ported from PRIMA.
- **[`SolisWets`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/solis_wets/struct.SolisWets.html)**:
  adaptive random local search: a randomized hill-climber testing `x + b + d`
  and `x − b − d` with `d ~ N(0, ρ²I)`, a success-direction bias `b`, and a step
  size `ρ` that expands after 5 successive successes and contracts after 3
  failures. The cheapest local search in the library (O(n) memory and time, cost
  evaluations only) and the classic memetic LS operator. Needs only a
  `CostFunction`. Implements Solis & Wets (1981).
- **[`Brent`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/brent/struct.Brent.html)**:
  robust 1-D minimization on a bracketed interval (Brent, 1973, as transcribed
  in *Numerical Recipes* §10.2); also used inside line searches.
- **[`GoldenSection`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/golden_section/struct.GoldenSection.html)**:
  golden-section search: 1-D minimization on a bracketed interval, the robust
  linearly-converging companion to `Brent` (Kiefer, 1953; *Numerical Recipes*
  §10.1).

## Quasi-Newton

- **[`Bfgs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bfgs/struct.Bfgs.html)**:
  dense quasi-Newton with a full approximate Hessian (Nocedal & Wright, 2006).
- **[`Lbfgs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/lbfgs/struct.Lbfgs.html)**:
  limited-memory BFGS for larger problems (two-loop recursion, Nocedal & Wright
  Alg. 7.4).
- **[`Lbfgsb`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/lbfgs/struct.Lbfgs.html)**:
  L-BFGS with box bounds; a faithful port of the Nocedal–Zhu L-BFGS-B v3.0
  Fortran source (Byrd, Lu & Nocedal, 1995; Zhu, Byrd, Lu & Nocedal, 1997, ACM
  TOMS Alg. 778).

## Trust Region

For problems exposing a `Hessian` or, matrix-free, a `HessianProduct` (either
analytic, or synthesized by `FiniteDiff`):

- **[`TrustRegion`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/trust_region/struct.TrustRegion.html)**:
  second-order trust-region Newton minimizer (Nocedal & Wright, 2006, Alg. 4.1).
  The subproblem strategy is pluggable: `Steihaug` (truncated CG, the default),
  `Dogleg`, `MoreSorensen` (the near-exact secular solve with hard-case
  treatment; Moré & Sorensen, 1983, “Computing a Trust Region Step”), or
  `CauchyPoint`.
  `TrustRegion::matrix_free()` drives the subproblem purely through
  Hessian-vector products (`HessianProduct`), never forming a matrix, so it
  scales to large problems (`Steihaug` and `CauchyPoint` only; `Dogleg` and
  `MoreSorensen` need the matrix).
  With ndarray, exact-Hessian mode requires a supplied `Array2` Hessian because
  `FiniteDiff` cannot construct one. Its matrix-free Hessian-vector products
  work on every backend.

## Nonlinear Least Squares

For problems expressed as residuals (`Residual` + `Jacobian`):

All five solvers accept
[`RobustLeastSquares`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/least_squares/struct.RobustLeastSquares.html)
to fit with Huber, soft-L1, Cauchy, arctangent, or custom losses and a residual
scale. The adapter adjusts the Gauss–Newton model while reporting the true
robust objective. It supports `f32` and `f64` on each solver's supported
backends and also exposes the objective to general optimizers. See the
[loss API and model](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/core/least_squares/index.html)
for the safeguarded curvature formula, following Triggs et al. (1999),
_Bundle Adjustment — A Modern Synthesis_, and SciPy's least-squares wrapper.

- **[`GaussNewton`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gauss_newton/struct.GaussNewton.html)**:
  undamped normal-equations solver (Madsen, Nielsen & Tingleff, 2004, §3.1).
- **[`LevenbergMarquardt`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/levenberg_marquardt/struct.LevenbergMarquardt.html)**:
  damped least squares; the workhorse for curve fitting. Marquardt (1963) with
  Nielsen's (1999) smooth damping update and Moré (1978)/MINPACK column scaling
  (Madsen, Nielsen & Tingleff, 2004, §3.2). Optional `LmDamping::TrustRegion`
  selects damping against a scaled radius, following MINPACK's
  [`lmder`](https://netlib.org/minpack/lmder.f) radius updates with a bracketed
  parameter search. Both damping strategies retain Basin's stopping tests.
- **[`LevenbergMarquardtQr`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/levenberg_marquardt/struct.LevenbergMarquardtQr.html)**:
  opt-in pivoted QR with the same configurable damping and stopping rules as
  `LevenbergMarquardt`. Avoids forming the Gram matrix; supports all four dense
  backends, with numerical rank checks after regularization. Sparse QR is not
  supported. Madsen, Nielsen & Tingleff (2004), _Methods for Non-Linear Least
  Squares Problems_, §3.2; MINPACK's
  [`qrfac`](https://netlib.org/minpack/qrfac.f) and
  [`qrsolv`](https://netlib.org/minpack/qrsolv.f) (Garbow, Hillstrom & Moré, 1980).
  Optional trust-region damping follows MINPACK's
  [`lmder`](https://netlib.org/minpack/lmder.f) radius updates, using bracketed
  parameter selection instead of [`lmpar`](https://netlib.org/minpack/lmpar.f).
- **[`Trf`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/trf/struct.Trf.html)**:
  simplified bounded Levenberg–Marquardt with Coleman–Li affine scaling
  (Branch, Coleman & Li, 1999). Its existing behavior and sparse support remain
  available through Basin 1.x.
- **[`TrustRegionReflective`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/trust_region_reflective/struct.TrustRegionReflective.html)**:
  dense trust-region-reflective least squares with an explicit radius, reflected
  steps, a rank-aware SVD solve, and fixed-parameter elimination. Supports all
  four dense backends in pure Rust, including `f32` and `f64`. Branch, Coleman &
  Li (1999), “A Subspace, Interior, and Conjugate Gradient Method for Large-Scale
  Bound-Constrained Minimization Problems,” _SIAM Journal on Scientific
  Computing_ 21(1), 1–23
  ([doi:10.1137/S1064827595289108](https://doi.org/10.1137/S1064827595289108));
  Moré (1978), “The Levenberg-Marquardt Algorithm: Implementation and Theory,”
  _Numerical Analysis_, Lecture Notes in Mathematics 630, 105–116
  ([doi:10.1007/BFb0067700](https://doi.org/10.1007/BFb0067700)). Reference
  comparisons use [SciPy 1.16.2's dense TRF](https://github.com/scipy/scipy/blob/v1.16.2/scipy/optimize/_lsq/trf.py).

## Global & Stochastic

- **[`Direct`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/direct/struct.Direct.html)**:
  original DIRECT for deterministic global optimization over finite box bounds.
  It selects potentially optimal rectangles by their center costs and
  half-diagonals, then trisects every longest axis. Fixed coordinates are
  eliminated, and `PointState` exposes the best sampled point. All four dense
  backends support `f32` and `f64`. Normalized radius stopping defaults to
  `1e-6`; relative volume stopping is opt-in. Cross-validated against SciPy
  1.16.2 with `locally_biased=False`.
  Reference: Donald R. Jones, Cary D. Perttunen, and Bruce E. Stuckman,
  “Lipschitzian optimization without the Lipschitz constant,”
  _Journal of Optimization Theory and Applications_ 79, 157–181 (1993),
  especially pp. 169–170, the subdivision procedure and Definition 4.1,
  [doi:10.1007/BF00941892](https://doi.org/10.1007/BF00941892).
- **[`Gbnm`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/gbnm/struct.Gbnm.html)**:
  Globalized Bounded Nelder-Mead over a feasible box. It combines projected
  local simplex searches with Parzen-density probabilistic restarts and the
  small-, flat-, and degenerate-simplex restart state machine. It retains the
  possible local optima found along the way and supports exact solver-aware
  resume. Implements M. A. Luersen and R. Le Riche, “Globalized Nelder–Mead
  method for engineering optimization,” _Computers & Structures_ 82 (2004),
  2251–2260
  ([doi:10.1016/j.compstruc.2004.03.072](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.compstruc.2004.03.072)).
- **[`SimulatedAnnealing`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/simulated_annealing/struct.SimulatedAnnealing.html)**:
  classical single-proposal simulated annealing with a user-supplied `Neighbor`,
  explicit geometric, reciprocal, or normalized-log cooling, and optional
  independently composable schedule restart triggers. It accepts arbitrary
  cloneable parameter types, including discrete structures, and supports exact
  solver-aware resume.
  Uses Metropolis et al. (1953; DOI 10.1063/1.1699114), implements Kirkpatrick,
  Gelatt & Vecchi (1983; DOI 10.1126/science.220.4598.671), and takes its
  logarithmic-schedule context from Hajek (1988; DOI 10.1287/moor.13.2.311).
- **[`CmaEs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/cma_es/struct.CmaEs.html)**/**[`BoundedCmaEs`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bounded_cma_es/struct.BoundedCmaEs.html)**:
  covariance-matrix adaptation evolution strategy (Hansen, 2016), unconstrained
  and box-bounded; the bounded variant uses Hansen's adaptive `BoundPenalty`
  (the `pycma` default).
- **[`De`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/de/struct.De.html)**:
  differential evolution (DE/rand/1/bin) over a feasible box (Storn & Price,
  1997).
- **[`GlobalBestPso`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/global_best_pso/struct.GlobalBestPso.html)**:
  synchronous inertia-weight global-best particle swarm optimization over a
  feasible box, with independent boundary-response and velocity-limit policies.
  Implements Kennedy & Eberhart's (1995; DOI 10.1109/ICNN.1995.488968) update
  with Shi & Eberhart's inertia formulation (1998; DOI
  10.1109/ICEC.1998.699146) and the Standard PSO 2006 coefficient/population
  profile (Bratton & Kennedy, 2007; DOI 10.1109/SIS.2007.368035), and is
  cross-validated against Argmin 0.11.0 commit
  `c94c32adefd6c2525ce05806092ca868ec85fba4`. It remains distinct from
  SPSO-2011 (Zambrano-Bigiarini, Clerc & Rojas, 2013; DOI
  10.1109/CEC.2013.6557848).
- **[`RandomSearch`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/random_search/struct.RandomSearch.html)**:
  elitist (1+λ) uniform sampling over a box.
- **[`Ssga`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ssga/struct.Ssga.html)**:
  steady-state real-coded genetic algorithm (replace-worst) with BLX-α crossover
  (Eshelman & Schaffer, 1993), negative assortative mating (Fernandes & Rosa,
  2001), and BGA mutation (Mühlenbein & Schlierkamp-Voosen, 1993); the SSGA
  component of Molina et al. (2010), §4.4.
- **[`BasinHopping`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/basin_hopping/struct.BasinHopping.html)**:
  basin-hopping (Wales & Doye, 1997): a Metropolis Monte-Carlo walk over the
  `Ẽ(x) = min{f(x)}` transform, wrapping any local solver with a pluggable step
  taker and acceptance test; adaptive step size on by default.
  Custom step takers or inner solvers can narrow backend support.

## Memetic (Composed)

These run a local solver inside a global one using Basin's solver composition
primitives. Custom inner solvers or local-search operators can narrow backend
support.

- **[`CmaInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/cma_inject/struct.CmaInject.html)**/**[`BoundedCmaInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/bounded_cma_inject/struct.BoundedCmaInject.html)**:
  CMA-ES with per-generation local polishing of the best individuals,
  re-injected via Hansen's (2011) injection mechanism.
- **[`DeInject`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/de_inject/struct.DeInject.html)**:
  the DE-flavored sibling of `CmaInject`: differential evolution (Storn & Price, 1997)
  with per-generation top-*k* local refinement and Hansen-style (2011)
  injection.
- **[`MaLsChCma`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ma_ls_ch/struct.MaLsCh.html)**:
  a memetic algorithm with persistent local-search chains and a CMA-ES inner
  (Molina et al., 2010).
- **[`MaLsChSw`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/ma_ls_ch/struct.MaLsCh.html)**:
  the high-dimensional sibling of `MaLsChCma`: the same local-search-chain
  framework with a Solis-Wets inner, whose O(n) chain snapshots keep
  chain-memetic search viable when the dimension grows (MA-SW-Chains: Molina,
  Lozano, and Herrera, 2010, winner of the CEC'2010 large-scale competition).
  Both are type aliases of the generic `MaLsCh<V, LS>`, which accepts any
  local-search operator implementing the `ResumableInner` (seed + snapshot +
  resume) trait.

## Constrained

- **[`Slsqp`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/slsqp/struct.Slsqp.html)**:
  dense Han–Powell sequential least-squares programming with damped BFGS,
  constrained least-squares subproblems, and an inexact L1 merit line search.
  Supports box bounds, linear and nonlinear equalities, and linear and nonlinear
  inequalities through `NonlinearConstraints` and `ConstraintJacobian`, with
  analytic or finite-difference derivatives. Reports feasibility, projected
  Lagrangian stationarity, and complementarity alongside Kraft's native
  convergence tests. Kraft, D. (1988), *A software package for sequential
  quadratic programming*, Technical Report DFVLR-FB 88-28, Institut für Dynamik
  der Flugsysteme, Oberpfaffenhofen, §§2.2 and 3.2
  ([report](https://degenerateconic.com/uploads/2018/03/DFVLR_FB_88_28.pdf)).
  Reference implementation: Jacob Williams's SLSQP 1.6.1, commit
  `97884f98042624007f736dc536fa5636906ff26a`, original NNLS and inexact line search.

The following wrap a gradient inner solver in an outer loop for linear
constraints:

- **[`BarrierMethod`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/barrier_method/struct.BarrierMethod.html)**:
  two-phase log-barrier interior-point continuation for linear inequality
  constraints (`A x ≤ b`); automatically finds a strictly feasible start when
  one exists (Boyd & Vandenberghe, *Convex Optimization*, §§11.3–11.4.1).
- **[`AugmentedLagrangianMethod`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/solver/augmented_lagrangian_method/struct.AugmentedLagrangianMethod.html)**:
  quadratic penalty plus multiplier updates for linear equality constraints
  (`A x = b`); tolerates an infeasible start (Nocedal & Wright, §17.3, Alg.
  17.4, LANCELOT-style).

The pluggable line searches are documented alongside the first-order solvers:
[`Backtracking`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/backtracking/struct.Backtracking.html)
(Armijo; Nocedal & Wright §3.1),
[`Wolfe`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/wolfe/struct.Wolfe.html)
(strong Wolfe; Nocedal & Wright Alg. 3.5/3.6),
[`MoreThuente`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/more_thuente/struct.MoreThuente.html)
(Moré & Thuente, 1994; a port of MINPACK-2's `dcsrch`), and
[`HagerZhang`](https://docs.rs/basin/latest/basin/line_search/hager_zhang/struct.HagerZhang.html)
(ordinary and approximate Wolfe; Hager & Zhang, 2005, DOI
10.1137/030601880).

## Backends and Constraints

All vector-based solvers support the four dense backends: `Vec`, nalgebra,
ndarray, and faer. Scalar algorithms need no linear-algebra backend. Each
solver's **Backends** section on [docs.rs](https://docs.rs/basin) describes its
supported scalar types, sparse support, and any additional requirements.

Box bounds and other constraints belong to the problem. Choose a solver that
supports those constraints, or use an explicit projection, barrier, or penalty
adapter.

See [Getting Started](../getting-started/) for a worked example, or open the
[Visualizer](../../visualizer/) to watch several of these solvers converge.
